 ## Background
-HOT-lines were introduced by Hlawitschka and Scheuermann [S1, book][S2]. The method generalizes streamlines (first-order tensors) and tensor lines (second-order tensors) to arbitrary even-order symmetric tensors. It was applied to MRI data to solve the tractography problem, specifically to address the limitation of second-order tensors in representing fiber crossings. The approach uses spherical harmonic representation for efficiency and relies on the implicit function theorem to ensure $C^1$ continuity of the tracked lines [S1, book][S2].
+HOT-lines were introduced by Hlawitschka and Scheuermann [S1, book][S2]. The method generalizes streamlines (first-order tensors) and tensor lines (second-order tensors) to arbitrary even-order symmetric tensors [S2]. It was applied to MRI data to solve the tractography problem, specifically to address the limitation of second-order tensors in representing fiber crossings [S1, book]. The approach uses spherical harmonic representation for efficiency and relies on the implicit function theorem to ensure $C^1$ continuity of the tracked lines [S1, book][S2].
 
